<!--
  The Google Play Games project number for this game.

  This is NOT optional once the Play Games SDK is on the classpath: the SDK
  initialises itself from a ContentProvider at app start and reads this value
  out of the manifest, so a build with the value missing crashes on launch
  rather than merely failing to sign in.

  The value below is the real project number for this game. It is not a
  secret: it ships inside every APK and identifies the game rather than
  authorising anything, which is why it lives in the repository next to the
  AdMob app id rather than in a keystore.

  Having it here is necessary but not sufficient. Play Games matches on the
  project, the package name and the signing certificate together, so a build
  signed with a certificate that has no credential against this project still
  fails to sign in. docs/play-games-setup.md has the whole job with the console
  screens named; the short version is:

    1. Play Console -> your app -> Grow -> Play Games Services -> Setup.
    2. Create a Play Games Services project. It gives you the project number
       above.
    3. Add a credential of type "Android", using the applicationId
       com.portalcrafter.pitchpole and the SHA-1 of the keystore the build is
       signed with. The debug keystore and the release keystore are different,
       so both need a credential or sign in will only work on one of them.

       Every machine generates its own debug keystore the first time it builds
       for Android, so this is per developer rather than per project. Read the
       one for the machine you are on with:

         keytool -list -v -keystore ~/.android/debug.keystore \
           -alias androiddebugkey -storepass android -keypass android

       Known debug certificates so far:
         58:59:5B:4C:80:AF:98:F6:EF:4E:52:21:A0:41:43:F4:C6:DF:CB:AF
         E7:D0:A5:6A:71:AC:89:FF:B8:D6:74:54:D2:2A:7A:BE:56:92:EA:A8

       Note that release builds currently sign with the debug keystore too,
       because android/app/build.gradle.kts has no release signing config yet.
       Once it gets one, that certificate needs a credential of its own, and
       an app shipped through Play App Signing needs the SHA-1 Google shows
       under Setup -> App integrity rather than the local one.
    3b. Add your own Google account to the Testers list on the Play Games
       Services project. Until the project is published, sign in fails for
       anybody not on that list even when everything above is correct.
    4. Put the project number here. Done.

  Whenever any of the above is incomplete the button on the home screen still
  appears; tapping it reports that Play Games is not set up rather than
  pretending to work.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="games_app_id" translatable="false">245388757681</string>
</resources>
